<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Australas. Chiropr. Osteopathy</journal-id><journal-title>Australasian Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathy</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1328-0384</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">17987153</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2050632</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE EFFECT OF SACROILIAC MANIPULATION ON HIP FLEXION RANGE OF MOTION</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Pollard</surname><given-names>Henry</given-names></name><degrees>B.Sc., Grad.Dip.(Chiro.), Grad.Dip.App.Sc., M.Sport.Sc.</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ward</surname><given-names>Graham</given-names></name><degrees>B.Sc., B.E., M.Sc., Ph.D.</degrees></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>11</month><year>1997</year></pub-date><volume>6</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>80</fpage><lpage>84</lpage><copyright-statement>Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathic College of Australasia</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1997</copyright-year><abstract><p>Chiropractors claim to be able to influence sites far removed from the point of application of spinal adjustment. Little scientific research has, however, been conducted showing conclusively that the spine and associated structures have an influence on distal function. Demonstration of such influence on distal tissues would aid in the scientific validation of Chiropractic by other health professionals and facilitate treatment of peripheral injuries such as hamstring strains. This study aimed to investigate the effect of a manipulation of the sacroiliac joint on the mechanical function of the hip joint. The results demonstrate that the sacroiliac joint manipulation did not statistically alter the range of motion of the hip joint.	  </p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Chiropractic</kwd><kwd>manipulation</kwd><kwd>hip</kwd><kwd>sacroiliac joint</kwd><kwd>range of motion</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>